{"path":"/documentation/schema/response","section":"documentation","title":"response","description":"Documentation for the nnsight Python library","toc":[{"id":"nnsight.schema.response","label":"response","depth":2},{"id":"nnsight.schema.response.RESULT","label":"RESULT\n\n\n  \n      module-attribute","depth":3},{"id":"nnsight.schema.response.Status","label":"Status","depth":3},{"id":"nnsight.schema.response.ResponseModel","label":"ResponseModel","depth":3}],"segments":[{"type":"html","html":"<h2 id=\"nnsight.schema.response\" class=\"doc-heading\">\n<code class=\"doc-symbol doc-symbol-heading doc-symbol-module\"></code>            <span class=\"doc-object-name doc-module-name\">response</span>\n\n\n</h2>"},{"type":"html","html":"<p>The status message a remote job sends back to the client.</p>\n<p>Each update NDIF pushes for a job — whether streamed over a blocking websocket or\nsaved to the object store and polled — arrives as a <a title=\"            ResponseModel\" href=\"#nnsight.schema.response.ResponseModel\"><code>ResponseModel</code></a>. Its\n<code>Status</code> names where the job is in its lifecycle; on <code>COMPLETED</code> the\nreturned values ride in <a title=\"            data\n\n\n  \n      class-attribute\n      instance-attribute\n  \" href=\"#nnsight.schema.response.ResponseModel.data\"><code>data</code></a>\nand what the run cost the server rides in\n<a title=\"            meta\n\n\n  \n      class-attribute\n      instance-attribute\n  \" href=\"#nnsight.schema.response.ResponseModel.meta\"><code>meta</code></a>.</p>"},{"type":"html","html":"<h3 id=\"nnsight.schema.response.RESULT\" class=\"doc-heading\">\n<code class=\"doc-symbol doc-symbol-heading doc-symbol-attribute\"></code>            <span class=\"doc-object-name doc-attribute-name\">RESULT</span>\n\n\n  <span class=\"doc-labels\">\n      <small class=\"doc-label doc-label-module-attribute\"><code>module-attribute</code></small>\n  </span>\n\n</h3>"},{"type":"code","lang":"python","code